1:# Structured Memory
3:## Summary
5:Phase 5 introduces a structured assertion layer on top of the existing durable memory store. The goal is not to replace freeform memory content, but to persist enough verification, provenance, freshness, and relationship metadata that later assistant workflows can reuse memory with an explainable trust order.
9:- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/model.rs` for the structured-memory types
10:- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/store.rs` for SQLite persistence, additive migration, default derivation, and recall ordering
11:- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/agent.rs` for assistant-facing ranking, highlight generation, and compact/full memory bundle shaping
15:## Why This Exists
17:Before Phase 5, memory rows were durable and searchable, but most trust judgment still had to be inferred from freeform text, confidence, and recency. That makes reuse fragile in longer coding sessions:
21:- an old memory can be contradicted or superseded without assistants seeing that clearly
22:- workflow outputs can reuse memory without telling the assistant why a memory should be trusted
24:The structured layer makes those states explicit so memory reuse can be both safer and easier to explain.
26:## Data Model
28:Each memory row still keeps the original core fields such as `content`, `memory_type`, `scope`, `confidence`, `linked_symbols`, `linked_files`, `workspace_id`, `branch`, `refresh_key`, and stale flags.
33:- `verification_status`
35:- `supersedes_memory_id`
36:- `superseded_by_memory_id`
37:- `contradicts_memory_ids`
38:- `contradicted_by_memory_ids`
39:- `freshness_policy`
40:- `freshness_policy_detail`
41:- `provenance`
42:- `evidence`
44:The assertion vocabulary is wider than the legacy `memory_type`. In addition to observation, decision, exploration, pattern, and anti-pattern, the structured layer also supports `workflow_outcome` and `constraint`.
46:The verification lifecycle currently supports:
52:- `contradicted`
53:- `superseded`
57:## Persistence And Migration
59:Structured memory is stored in the existing `memories` SQLite table. The schema is extended additively with new scalar columns and JSON columns for contradiction edges, provenance, and evidence.
65:- new indexes are added for `verification_status` and `superseded_by_memory_id`
68:This means old databases continue to open without a destructive rewrite. Legacy rows receive column defaults at the database level. Structured defaults are then derived on later store/update paths when Lattice rewrites a memory row.
70:## Default Structured Derivation
76:- extended `assertion_type` values such as `workflow_outcome` and `constraint` are preserved instead of being reset from the legacy `memory_type`
77:- legacy-compatible assertion types continue to follow `memory_type`
78:- scope-derived freshness policies are recomputed from the current scope
79:- `stale` is forced if `memory.is_stale` is true
80:- a previously stale row falls back to inferred verification when it is no longer stale
81:- default provenance and evidence are filled in if missing
85:- `assertion_type` comes from `memory_type`
86:- `verification_status` is inferred from row state
87:- `freshness_policy` comes from scope
88:- `confidence_reason` is synthesized from a verification-like `source_query` or low confidence
89:- provenance is seeded from `source_query`, `refresh_key`, or an `assistant_observation` fallback
90:- evidence is seeded from linked symbols and linked files
92:The current verification inference is conservative:
95:- rows whose `source_query` contains verification signals such as `verified`, `validated`, `from code`, `from tests`, or `code and tests` become `verified`
99:## Supersession, Contradiction, And Staleness Semantics
103:### Staleness
109:- `verification_status = 'stale'`
111:Refreshing, promoting, or directly updating content clears the stale flags. If the previous verification state was only stale, the store drops it back to `unverified` unless stronger structured metadata is reapplied later.
113:### Supersession
115:Supersession means a row has been replaced by a newer or stronger memory. `mark_memory_superseded` records the replacing memory id in `superseded_by_memory_id` and sets `verification_status` to `superseded`.
117:### Contradiction
119:Contradiction means two memories disagree. `mark_memory_contradicted`:
121:- appends the newer contradicting id to the older row’s `contradicted_by_memory_ids`
122:- marks the older row as `contradicted`
123:- backfills the reverse edge by appending the older id to the newer row’s `contradicts_memory_ids`
125:These states are intentionally separate. A row can be stale because the code changed, superseded because a better replacement exists, or contradicted because another memory disagrees.
127:## Recall And Trust Ordering
131:### Store-Level Recall
133:`find_by_refresh_key` is the store’s durable recall path for a scoped memory identity. It prefers memories in this order:
139:5. `superseded`
141:7. `contradicted`
145:- rows that are not superseded
154:### Assistant-Facing Ordering
156:Assistant-facing bundles in `agent.rs` apply a richer ranking to memory values before surfacing them as highlights or compact memory objects.
160:- effective verification status
161:- memory scope, with `repo` above `branch` above `session`
163:- counts of provenance and evidence entries
165:- presence of supersession or contradiction links
167:The effective verification status intentionally overrides raw status when stronger trust signals are visible in the value:
169:- any `contradicted_by_memory_ids` makes the row effectively `contradicted`
170:- any `superseded_by_memory_id` makes it effectively `superseded`
172:- otherwise the explicit `verification_status` is used
174:This keeps downstream assistant ranking aligned with the relationship and freshness markers even if a caller passes partially normalized memory values.
176:## Assistant-Facing Bundle Use
180:First, it emits `memory_highlights` that carry a compact explanation surface:
183:- legacy memory type
187:- effective verification status
189:- freshness policy and detail
191:Those highlights are used directly in workflow overviews. Verified memories are phrased as reusable guidance such as `reuse verified repo workflow outcome`, while stale, superseded, and contradicted memories are phrased as notes instead of reusable advice.
195:- assertion and verification fields
196:- contradiction and supersession ids
197:- freshness policy fields
198:- truncated provenance
199:- truncated evidence
201:Compact mode intentionally omits the full `memories` payload and keeps only highlights. Full mode keeps up to two compact memory entries.
210:## Explainability Boundary
212:The structured-memory design is meant to support explainable reuse, not opaque scoring. The current implementation keeps that boundary by exposing the trust factors that changed ranking:
214:- verification status
218:- freshness policy
219:- provenance
220:- evidence
221:- contradiction and supersession links
223:That gives assistant workflows a basis for saying why a memory is being reused or why it should be treated only as a warning.
225:## Current Limits
230:- legacy rows are migrated additively, but structured provenance and evidence are only fully derived when the row is stored or otherwise updated again
231:- this note does not freeze any daemon or MCP wire shape for structured memory fields; active daemon-facing payload details should be documented separately once that worker-side contract is settled
233:## Verification Status
240:- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/model.rs`
241:- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/store.rs`
242:- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/tests.rs`
248:- structured assertion enums and fields exist in the memory model
252:- staleness, supersession, and contradiction are stored as distinct states
253:- assistant-facing memory ordering, highlights, and full-mode compact memory payloads use the structured trust signals described above

